  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "The oldest map of America was drawn with modern paint. Yale unveiled it in 1965, a day before Columbus Day, showing Vinland southwest of Greenland.",
        "visual_query": "old world map parchment chart",
        "visual_fallback": "antique map"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Paul Mellon had bought it for roughly three hundred thousand dollars and donated it, on one condition: total secrecy until the scholars published.",
        "visual_query": "rare book library reading room",
        "visual_fallback": "old library"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It looked unbeatable. So why did chemist after chemist keep finding a pigment no medieval scribe could possibly have owned?",
        "visual_query": "medieval manuscript vellum writing closeup",
        "visual_fallback": "old manuscript"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The ink held anatase, a titanium dioxide manufactured only from the 1920s onward. Walter McCrone's team flagged it back in 1972.",
        "visual_query": "laboratory microscope pigment analysis",
        "visual_fallback": "science laboratory"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Yale settled it in 2021. Titanium everywhere, almost no iron gall ink, and modern writing added to the real medieval manuscript bound beside it.",
        "visual_query": "norse greenland coastline ice fjord",
        "visual_fallback": "icy coastline"
      }
    ],
